{"conditionalSetIds":[],"forecasterId":"demographics-ai-2036:run2:inflation:scout","informationSetArtifactId":"sha256:6a69398e7ee702862309df3827cf34c2cbebe6766195a13369d40f346f223fa1","modelForecastIds":[],"modelUse":"none","prediction":{"kind":"binary","positiveOutcomeId":"yes","probability":0.34},"questionHash":"sha256:a11e957a2fbf9e72130705a8815d67b5a6c66c3444a8332ee92cdcb4248068e1","questionId":"demographics-ai-2036-cq2","questionVersion":"1.0.0","rationale":"THE LEVEL/SKEW SEPARATION IS THE WHOLE ANSWER HERE. Carry the weights.\n\nCONJUNCT 1 — GDP-weighted OECD-38 core CPI/PCE avg >=2.50%/yr over 2027-2036: marginal 62%. OBSERVED (hard administrative): US core PCE +3.1% y/y September 2026 (BEA release 29 October 2026), +0.3% m/m, \"the same pace as a revised reading for August\"; prior month 3.0%. One secondary outlet reported 3.6% — that is HEADLINE PCE, not core, and I am not using it as core. Euro area: October 2026 flash was scheduled for 4 November 2026 and is unverified at my horizon; latest euro-area headline found is 3.8% y/y for September 2026 \"against a forecast of 3.6%\" (secondary aggregator — the results \"conflict on the timing of the euro area's latest data\", some pages saying the last confirmed reading was July 2026). National hard flashes for October 2026: Germany headline +2.8%, core ex food/energy +2.1% (Destatis); Spain HICP headline 4.9%, core 3.1% (INE). So the START is 2.1-3.1% core across the big weights, and US core PCE has printed >=2.5% every year 2021-2026.\nWEIGHTED SKEW ARITHMETIC (my effort's core requirement): OECD core baskets are shelter- and services-heavy — shelter alone is ~30-33% of US core CPI and ~17-18% of core PCE; medical + other administered services add roughly another 15-20%. Three blocs: (a) shelter + care + construction-intensive + electricity-exposed services, weight ~0.45-0.55, running 3.5-5.0%; (b) software/media/communications/professional services, weight ~0.20-0.25, the AI-deflation bloc, 0.0-1.0%; (c) tradable goods, weight ~0.25, 1.0-2.0% with tariff pass-through offsetting China export deflation. Hot mix: 0.50x4.5 + 0.25x1.5 + 0.25x2.0 = 3.1%. Cold mix: 0.45x3.5 + 0.30x0.5 + 0.25x1.5 = 2.1%. The decade average lands >=2.50% in most mixes because AI CANNOT DISINFLATE RENT, and the 2027-2030 impulse leg is observable now, not extrapolated: US residential electricity rates rose 7.3% between April 2025 and April 2026 (EIA data via secondary), EIA STEO has the 2026 residential average at 18.2 c/kWh with commercial sales (incl. data centres) growing \"about 2.2% in 2026 and 5.3% in 2027\" against residential sales growth \"only about 0.5% a year\", and commercial demand surpassing residential \"for the first time on record in 2027.\" CAVEAT VERBATIM, and it matters: the PJM independent market monitor \"attributed roughly 63% of the 2025-2026 auction price surge to AI-focused data center demand\", but an EPRI study \"found that between 2015 and 2024, data center growth in the United States modestly reduced average retail electricity rates, which conflicts with the PJM findings\", and PolitiFact rated a June 2026 claim about bills rising up to 267% near data centers \"Mostly False.\" The attribution is contested; the rate increase itself is not. Also note \"Utilities cite higher fuel prices for generation and expenses for bolstering the transmission grid\" — not only data centres. Front-loading arithmetic: 2027-2030 at ~2.9% leaves 2031-2036 needing only >=2.23% to clear 2.50%. 62%.\n\nCONJUNCT 2 — China headline CPI avg <1.50%/yr over 2027-2036: marginal 52%, and this is where my prior MOVED AGAINST the consensus deflation call. OBSERVED (hard, NBS via Mysteel): China CPI +0.9% y/y September 2026; +1.0% y/y June 2026. October 2026 is not out — NBS releases around 9 November and today is 4 November 2026, so it is unverified at my horizon; most search hits for \"October CPI\" are October 2025 (CPI +0.2%, PPI -2.1%, \"the 37th straight month of decline\") and are the WRONG YEAR. THE CRITICAL SIGNAL: producer prices have flipped POSITIVE. Mysteel reports September 2026 PPI +5.0% y/y and June 2026 PPI +4.1% y/y. I COULD NOT CONFIRM the +5.0% against a second source — \"I couldn't find any source reporting China's September 2026 PPI at +5%, so I can't confirm that figure\" — treat as single-source and unverified. It is corroborated in DIRECTION by T. Rowe Price (April 2026 institutional outlook), which argues \"the oil price jump resulting from the Middle East conflict helped flip the change in PPI into positive territory in March 2026 given the close correlation of Chinese PPI and oil prices\" and expects \"the year-over-year change in China's producer price index (PPI) will remain positive through 2026\", under the title \"As China's Disinflation Fades, Inflation Risks May Rise Globally.\" Treating Chinese CPI as a SEMI-INDEPENDENT object as instructed: the three drags are real and observable — new home prices \"have fallen year-on-year for 38 consecutive months\", property's share of household assets down \"from 67% in mid-2021 to 52%\", household consumption only ~39.6-40% of GDP, the 15th Five-Year Plan \"calls for raising the share\" but \"they have not set a specific target\", Beijing \"has largely resisted economists' calls for fiscal stimulus\", and Continuum sees residential property \"adverse to GDP directly and indirectly (circa 1% in 2026 and 2027)\". Against them, over a TEN-year window: the locked 2A contraction (population -60M 2026-35, worker/retiree 4.4 -> 2.8) turns China from a saving-glut deflation exporter into a labour-scarce dissaver by the early 2030s, the anti-involution capacity consolidation is explicitly a price-RAISING policy, and oil/food/RMB pass-through is live. A 10-year average <1.50% requires the 2023-2026 regime (~0.2-1.0%) to persist through a window in which the demographic mechanics flip sign. One analyst caveat kept verbatim: \"PPI will stay under pressure until the property market stabilizes and consumer confidence recovers\", adding \"the campaign alone is unlikely to change the trend.\" 52%.\n\nCORRELATION: approximately zero, with a small POSITIVE channel I will name rather than assume away — tariffs/fragmentation simultaneously RAISE OECD goods prices and LOWER Chinese domestic prices (diverted export supply stays home and gluts the domestic market), which is exactly the joint cell the question asks for. Offsetting that, a common global energy/commodity impulse lifts both. Net r ~ +0.05. JOINT: 0.62 x 0.52 = 0.322, x1.05 = 0.338 -> 34.","referenceClassIds":[],"schemaVersion":"forecast-workbench.forecast/v1","sealedAt":"2026-11-04T17:31:56.654Z","seriesId":"published-panel","strongestContraryCase":"Not elicited per question. Panelist falsification signposts: {\"signpost\":\"US core PCE 12-month rate for December 2028 (BEA personal income and outlays release, late January 2029). Observed anchor: 3.1% y/y for September 2026, +0.3% m/m, BEA release 29 October 2026.\",\"by_when\":\"2029-02-15\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"If <2.2%, my front-loaded 2027-2030 inflationary impulse is wrong and CQ2 conjunct 1 collapses: cut CQ2 from 34 to ~22, move ~6 points of CQ6 mass from C to D, and lower CQ4 P50 by ~0.2pp. If >=3.2%, raise CQ2 to ~44, shift ~5 points from B to C, and raise CQ4 P90 toward 4.0%.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"EIA average US residential electricity price for calendar 2029 (Electric Power Monthly / STEO annual), against the 18.2 c/kWh 2026 average, together with whether EIA's forecast that commercial demand surpasses residential in 2027 actually realizes.\",\"by_when\":\"2030-04-30\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"If the cumulative 2026-2029 nominal increase is <8% (roughly flat in real terms), the electricity pass-through leg of my hot-impulse story is falsified: cut CQ6-C by ~5 points into B, cut CQ2 by ~5, and lower my coupled AI-Q3 downgrade (the retail-rate veto on load growth would be absent). If >25% cumulative, raise CQ6-C by ~5 and CQ2 by ~4.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"China full-year headline CPI for 2029 (NBS, mid-January 2030), plus whether annual PPI stays positive in 2027, 2028 and 2029. Observed anchor: CPI +0.9% y/y September 2026, +1.0% June 2026; PPI positive since March 2026 (single-source +5.0% September 2026, unconfirmed).\",\"by_when\":\"2030-02-15\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"If 2029 CPI >=2.0% or PPI has been positive in all three years, CQ2's China conjunct is failing: cut CQ2 from 34 to ~18. If 2029 CPI <=0.5% with PPI back negative, raise CQ2 to ~42 and shift ~3 points of CQ6 mass from C to B (China resumes exporting goods deflation into OECD core's tradable bloc).\"} | {\"signpost\":\"ICE BofA MOVE Index calendar-year average for 2029, against the ~66-71 observed through 2026 (vendor index; no administrative equivalent exists).\",\"by_when\":\"2030-01-31\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"If the 2029 average is <80, CQ5 conjunct 1 is on a failing path: cut CQ5 from 13 to ~6 and lower CQ4 P90 by ~0.3pp. If >=110, raise CQ5 to ~25, raise CQ4 P50 by ~0.3pp to 2.4%, and move ~4 points of CQ6 mass into C.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"Cumulative BLS ECI wage-and-salary growth differential, (health care and social assistance + construction) minus (information + financial activities + professional/scientific/technical), 2025Q4 to 2030Q4 — with the Eurostat Labour Cost Index F+Q vs J+K+M differential as the cross-check. Note this differential is effectively UNMEASURED in my current telemetry.\",\"by_when\":\"2031-03-31\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"A >=10pp move by 2036 needs roughly +4.5pp cumulative by end-2030. If the realized differential is <+2.5pp, CQ1 drops from 10 to ~5. If it is >=+6pp, raise CQ1 to ~22 and raise CQ6-B by ~3 points (the barbell is confirmed in wages, not just in prices).\"}","updateBasis":{"kind":"launch","note":"run2 wave 1 scout seat, effort 'inflation'."}}